WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.376 --> 00:00:06.211
All right. Good afternoon, y’all.

00:00:06.253 --> 00:00:08.964
Let's talk about blending
reality and imagination.

00:00:10.006 --> 00:00:14.511
But first, let's take
a step back in time to 2001.

00:00:14.553 --> 00:00:16.012
As an 11-year-old in India,

00:00:16.054 --> 00:00:20.267
I became obsessed
with computer graphics and visual effects.

00:00:20.308 --> 00:00:21.601
Of course, at that age,

00:00:21.601 --> 00:00:24.145
it meant making cheesy videos
kind of like this.

00:00:24.896 --> 00:00:28.066
But therein started
a foundational theme in my life,

00:00:28.066 --> 00:00:31.695
the quest to blend reality
and imagination.

00:00:31.695 --> 00:00:33.530
And that quest has stayed with me

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:36.575
and permeated across
my decade-long career in tech,

00:00:36.575 --> 00:00:39.828
working as a product manager
at companies like Google

00:00:39.869 --> 00:00:43.999
and as a content creator
on platforms like YouTube and TikTok.

00:00:44.833 --> 00:00:49.629
So today, let's deconstruct this quest
to blend reality and imagination

00:00:49.671 --> 00:00:53.008
and explore how it’s getting 
supercharged -- buzzword alert --

00:00:53.049 --> 00:00:54.759
by artificial intelligence.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:56.970
Let's start with the reality bit.

00:00:58.638 --> 00:01:01.016
You probably heard about photogrammetry.

00:01:01.057 --> 00:01:04.686
It's the art and science
of measuring stuff in the real world

00:01:04.686 --> 00:01:07.355
using photos and other sensors.

00:01:07.355 --> 00:01:12.402
What required massive data centers
and teams of experts in the 2000s

00:01:12.402 --> 00:01:16.323
became increasingly
democratized by the 2010s.

00:01:16.323 --> 00:01:18.658
Then, of course,
machine learning came along

00:01:18.658 --> 00:01:20.785
and took things to a whole new level

00:01:20.827 --> 00:01:25.081
with techniques like
neural radiance fields, or NeRFs.

00:01:25.123 --> 00:01:27.917
What you're seeing here is an AI model

00:01:27.959 --> 00:01:31.421
creating a ground-up
volumetric 3D representation

00:01:31.463 --> 00:01:33.715
using 2D images alone.

00:01:33.757 --> 00:01:36.676
But unlike older techniques
for reality capture,

00:01:36.718 --> 00:01:41.598
NeRFs do a really good job
of encapsulating the sheer complexity

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:43.516
and nuance of reality.

00:01:43.558 --> 00:01:45.518
The vibe, if you will.

00:01:46.311 --> 00:01:48.855
Twelve months later,
you can do all of this stuff

00:01:48.855 --> 00:01:50.607
using the iPhone in your pocket,

00:01:50.607 --> 00:01:52.484
using apps like Luma.

00:01:52.484 --> 00:01:55.362
It's like 3D screenshots
for the real world.

00:01:55.362 --> 00:01:59.866
Capture anything once and reframe it
infinitely in postproduction,

00:01:59.866 --> 00:02:04.162
so you can start building that collection
of spaces, places and objects

00:02:04.204 --> 00:02:05.789
that you truly care about

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:08.416
and conjure them up
in your future creations.

00:02:09.751 --> 00:02:11.503
So that's the reality bit.

00:02:11.544 --> 00:02:13.922
As NeRFs were popping off last year,

00:02:13.963 --> 00:02:16.841
the AI summer was also in full effect,

00:02:16.883 --> 00:02:20.345
with Midjourney, DALL-E 2,
Stable Diffusion

00:02:20.387 --> 00:02:23.056
all hitting the market
around the same time.

00:02:23.098 --> 00:02:26.351
But what I fell in love with
was inpainting.

00:02:26.351 --> 00:02:29.437
This technique allows you
to take existing imagery

00:02:29.437 --> 00:02:31.856
and augment it with whatever you like,

00:02:31.856 --> 00:02:35.193
and the results are
photorealistically fantastic.

00:02:35.193 --> 00:02:36.486
It blew my mind

00:02:36.486 --> 00:02:40.699
because stuff that would have taken me
like three hours in classical workflows

00:02:40.699 --> 00:02:43.576
I could pull off in just three minutes.

00:02:44.285 --> 00:02:45.537
But I wanted more.

00:02:45.578 --> 00:02:46.830
Enter ControlNet,

00:02:46.871 --> 00:02:49.833
a game-changing technique
by Stanford researchers

00:02:49.874 --> 00:02:53.211
that allows you to use
various input conditions

00:02:53.253 --> 00:02:57.257
to guide and control the
AI image generation process.

00:02:57.298 --> 00:03:00.510
So in my case, I could take
the depth information

00:03:00.552 --> 00:03:03.513
and the texture detail from my 3D scans

00:03:03.555 --> 00:03:06.725
and use it to literally reskin reality.

00:03:06.766 --> 00:03:08.309
Now, this isn't just cool video.

00:03:08.309 --> 00:03:10.603
There’s a lot of useful use cases, too.

00:03:10.603 --> 00:03:16.109
For example, in this case I'm taking
a 3D scan of my parents' drawing room,

00:03:16.109 --> 00:03:17.694
as my mother likes to call it,

00:03:17.694 --> 00:03:21.698
and reskinning it
to different styles of Indian decor

00:03:21.698 --> 00:03:25.910
and doing so while respecting
the spatial context and the layout

00:03:25.952 --> 00:03:28.037
of the interior space.

00:03:28.079 --> 00:03:30.331
If you squint, I'm sure you can see

00:03:30.373 --> 00:03:32.709
how this is going
to transform architecture

00:03:32.751 --> 00:03:34.961
and interior design forever.

00:03:35.503 --> 00:03:38.715
You could take that 2016 scan
of a Buddha statue

00:03:38.757 --> 00:03:41.134
and reskin it to be gloriously golden

00:03:41.176 --> 00:03:43.720
while pulling off
these impossible camera moves

00:03:43.762 --> 00:03:45.805
you just couldn't do any other way.

00:03:46.765 --> 00:03:50.602
Or you could take that vacation footage
from your trip to Tokyo

00:03:50.602 --> 00:03:54.189
and bring these cherry blossoms to life
in a whole new way.

00:03:54.189 --> 00:03:57.484
And let me tell you, cherry blossoms
look really good during the day,

00:03:57.484 --> 00:04:00.236
but they look even better at night.

00:04:00.236 --> 00:04:02.739
Oh, my God. They sure are glowing.

00:04:02.739 --> 00:04:04.657
It's almost like this dreamlike quality

00:04:04.657 --> 00:04:09.329
where you can use AI to accentuate
the best aspects of the real world.

00:04:10.205 --> 00:04:12.582
Natural landscapes look just as beautiful.

00:04:12.624 --> 00:04:15.835
Like this waterfall that
could be on another planet.

00:04:15.877 --> 00:04:18.505
But of course, you could go
over the hills and far away

00:04:18.546 --> 00:04:20.548
to the French Alps from another dimension.

00:04:21.883 --> 00:04:23.843
But it's not just static scenes.

00:04:23.885 --> 00:04:26.763
You can do this stuff with video, too.

00:04:26.805 --> 00:04:30.725
I can't wait till this technology
is running at 30 frames per second

00:04:30.767 --> 00:04:34.479
because it's going to transform
augmented reality and 3D rendering.

00:04:34.479 --> 00:04:38.066
I mean, how soon until
we're channel-surfing realities

00:04:38.066 --> 00:04:40.527
layered on top of the real world?

00:04:41.236 --> 00:04:43.863
Of course, just like reality capture
got democratized,

00:04:43.863 --> 00:04:47.325
all these tools from last year
are getting even easier.

00:04:47.325 --> 00:04:49.661
So instead of me spending hours

00:04:49.702 --> 00:04:51.871
weaving together
a bunch of different tools,

00:04:51.913 --> 00:04:56.084
tools like Runway and Kaiber
let you do exactly the same stuff

00:04:56.125 --> 00:04:57.710
with just a couple clicks.

00:04:57.752 --> 00:05:00.672
Want to go from day to night? No problemo.

00:05:00.713 --> 00:05:04.008
Want to get that retro 90s aesthetic
from "Full House"?

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:05.510
You can do that too.

00:05:06.427 --> 00:05:09.055
But it goes beyond reality capture.

00:05:09.097 --> 00:05:10.974
Companies like Wonder Dynamics

00:05:11.015 --> 00:05:15.478
are turning video into this immaculate
form of performance capture

00:05:15.478 --> 00:05:20.441
so you can embody fantastical creatures
using the phone in your pocket.

00:05:20.441 --> 00:05:24.070
This is stuff that James Cameron
only dreamt about in the 2000s.

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:27.407
And now you could do it with your iPhone?
That’s absolutely wild to me.

00:05:28.241 --> 00:05:31.870
So when I look back
at the past two decades

00:05:31.911 --> 00:05:36.040
and this ill-tailored tapestry
of tools that I've had to learn,

00:05:36.082 --> 00:05:38.918
I feel a sense of optimism
for what lies ahead

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:40.712
for the next generation of creators.

00:05:41.504 --> 00:05:43.506
The 11-year-olds of today

00:05:43.548 --> 00:05:45.842
don't have to worry
about all of that crap.

00:05:45.884 --> 00:05:48.845
All they need to do
is have a creative vision

00:05:48.887 --> 00:05:52.223
and a knack for working in concert
with these AI models,

00:05:52.265 --> 00:05:55.101
these AI models
that are truly a distillation

00:05:55.101 --> 00:05:57.687
of human knowledge and creativity.

00:05:57.687 --> 00:05:59.689
And that's a future I'm excited about,

00:05:59.689 --> 00:06:02.901
a future where you can blend
reality and imagination

00:06:02.901 --> 00:06:04.819
with your trusty AI copilot.

00:06:05.361 --> 00:06:06.654
Thank you very much.

00:06:06.654 --> 00:06:08.740
(Applause)

